    "sKids": "Multiple applicants proposed .kids in 2000; one application also paired it with .xxx.",
    "iKids": "The early Web was already asking whether children needed a distinct, governed space rather than merely filtered content.",
    "sHealth": "WHO proposed a dedicated .health namespace.",
    "iHealth": "Long before today’s arguments about online misinformation, public-health actors were already thinking about trust at the naming layer.",
    "sUnion": "The International Confederation of Free Trade Unions applied for .union.",
    "iUnion": "Industrial-era collective institutions were actively looking for a native place in the new digital public sphere.",
    "sNyc": "A .nyc application existed in 2000 and was later withdrawn.",
    "iNyc": "The desire for city identity online appeared before ICANN had a mature framework for city TLDs.",
    "sSucks": "Name.Space proposed .sucks in 2000; a .sucks gTLD was eventually delegated in 2015.",
    "iSucks": "A provocative idea moved from fringe proposal to production infrastructure. The boundary of what “belongs” in the root expanded dramatically.",
    "sWar": ".war appeared in the huge Name.Space bundle of proposed strings.",
    "iWar": "The proposal treated the DNS almost as a taxonomy of human life, including its darkest categories.",
    "sSoup": "Yes: .soup was proposed.",
    "iSoup": "The early expansion debate sometimes assumed that almost any semantic category could deserve a top-level namespace.",
    "sMagic": ".magic was proposed in 2000.",
    "iMagic": "Early Internet naming still carried a distinctly utopian belief that broad concepts could become self-contained digital places.",
    "sMobi": ".mobi was proposed in the 2004 sponsored round and later delegated.",
    "iMobi": "Before responsive design and smartphones normalized one Web, mobile looked different enough to deserve its own namespace.",
    "sMail": ".mail was proposed as a sponsored namespace but was not delegated.",
    "iMail": "Spam and trust were already serious enough problems that some actors tried to solve them at the naming layer.",
    "sCat": ".cat was created for the Catalan linguistic and cultural community.",
    "iCat": "A TLD did not need to map neatly onto a sovereign state: culture and language could also become infrastructure.",
    "sPost": ".post came from the Universal Postal Union.",
    "iPost": "One of the oldest global communications infrastructures tried to map itself into the architecture of the Internet.",
    "sXxx": ".xxx became one of the most contentious sponsored-TLD stories.",
    "iXxx": "Naming decisions are not merely technical; they can become proxies for wider political, cultural and regulatory conflicts.",
    "sGuru": ".guru was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iGuru": "The 2010s turned expertise itself into a marketable online identity.",
    "sNinja": ".ninja was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iNinja": "The playful job-title vocabulary of startup and developer culture reached the DNS root.",
    "sRich": ".rich was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iRich": "Status and exclusivity became explicit identity categories that could be bought and signalled online.",
    "sDating": ".dating was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iDating": "As platforms specialized, intimate life also became a commercial vertical in the namespace.",
    "sGop": ".gop was delegated to a US political organization.",
    "iGop": "Political identity itself became a branded namespace, showing how far the 2012 round pushed specialization.",
    "sLgbt": ".lgbt was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iLgbt": "Community and identity could be represented directly at the top level of the naming system.",
    "sBible": ".bible was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iBible": "Faith communities joined brands, places and industries in claiming explicit space in the root.",
    "sBeer": ".beer was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iBeer": "Products were no longer only things sold online; they became lifestyle categories with their own namespaces.",
    "sPizza": ".pizza was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iPizza": "Everyday consumption became part of the DNS vocabulary, from sectors and brands down to food culture.",
    "sWtf": ".wtf was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iWtf": "Internet slang itself became infrastructure, perhaps the most concise proof that the namespace had become culturally mainstream.",
    "sFail": ".fail was delegated from the 2012 round.",
    "iFail": "Even failure became a viable semantic category in the DNS root. The namespace had moved far beyond institutional labels.",
